  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `{cite}` of `requirements.authoring.usage_storage` on `src/authoring/usage.rs#struct[UsageLog]` is the right role.\n\n- **Not a test.** `UsageLog` is a plain struct, so `{test}` does not apply.\n- **Not an `{impl}` root.** The struct is a runtime handle. It holds the in-memory `record: UsageRecord`, the `path` the record is saved to, and bookkeeping fields: `started`, `seen`, `active_request` and the `first_request_finished` callback. None of its fields constrain the format version, run, project root, subject, outcome, determination or evidence fields that the requirement lists.\n- **Where the obligations are met.** The record's serialized fields are on `struct[UsageRecord]`. The `<state-root>/authoring/usage/<run-id>.json` path is built in `impl[UsageLog]/fn[start]`. The requirement is implemented in `src/authoring/mod.rs`, in `fn[prepare_assessment]`, `fn[author_evidence_with_output]`, `PendingAssessment/fn[persisted]` and `PendingAssessment/fn[failed]`, which already carry `{impl}` references to it.\n- **`{cite}` fits.** The struct is the handle for the per-assessment JSON record at the requirement's location, and its `path` and `record` fields would have to agree with a change to the requirement. It has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same requirement, so the citation is not redundant.\n\nI found no gap.",
